Every major Java framework uses reflection. Understanding the pattern demystifies 'magic' features.

**Spring — `@Autowired` field injection**:

```java
for (Field f : beanClass.getDeclaredFields()) {
    if (f.isAnnotationPresent(Autowired.class)) {
        f.setAccessible(true);
        Object dependency = applicationContext.getBean(f.getType());
        f.set(beanInstance, dependency);
    }
}
```

The bean class doesn't implement any special interface — Spring reflects on field annotations and injects matching beans from its container.

**Jackson — POJO serialization**:

```java
for (Field f : obj.getClass().getDeclaredFields()) {
    JsonProperty annotation = f.getAnnotation(JsonProperty.class);
    String jsonName = annotation != null ? annotation.value() : f.getName();
    f.setAccessible(true);
    json.put(jsonName, f.get(obj));
}
```

**Hibernate / JPA — entity mapping**:

```java
for (Field f : entityClass.getDeclaredFields()) {
    Column col = f.getAnnotation(Column.class);
    String columnName = col != null ? col.name() : f.getName();
    // Generate SELECT / UPDATE statements dynamically
}
```

**Mockito — mock creation**:
Mockito uses reflection (and bytecode manipulation via ByteBuddy) to create a subclass of your mocked type at runtime, overriding all methods to return default values until you stub them.

**JUnit — test discovery**:

```java
for (Method m : testClass.getDeclaredMethods()) {
    if (m.isAnnotationPresent(Test.class)) {
        m.setAccessible(true);
        runTest(testInstance, m);
    }
}
```

In every case, the framework doesn't know your types at compile time — reflection is the bridge that lets generic framework code work with any user-defined class.

**Performance**:

- `Method.invoke()`: ~10-100x slower than direct call (warm JIT), due to argument boxing, permission checks, and indirect dispatch
- `Field.get/set`: ~10x slower than direct access
- `MethodHandle.invoke()`: ~2-3x slower than direct — often JIT-inlined away entirely
- `Class.forName()`: expensive the first time; cached in the ClassLoader afterward

For reflection in a hot loop, cache the `Field` or `Method` object:

```java
// BAD — resolves the method on every call
for (int i = 0; i < 1_000_000; i++) {
    Method m = obj.getClass().getDeclaredMethod("process");
    m.invoke(obj);
}

// GOOD — resolve once, reuse
Method m = Target.class.getDeclaredMethod("process");
m.setAccessible(true);
for (int i = 0; i < 1_000_000; i++) {
    m.invoke(obj);
}
```

**Type safety**:
Reflection bypasses generics and compile-time checks. `method.invoke(obj, args)` takes `Object...` — you can pass the wrong types and get `ClassCastException` or `IllegalArgumentException` at runtime. No IDE autocomplete, no refactoring support.

**Encapsulation**:
`setAccessible(true)` bypasses `private` — reflection can read anything. This breaks encapsulation contracts; libraries should only use it when necessary. **In Java 9+ with the module system**, `setAccessible(true)` is restricted on JDK-internal classes. On Java 17+, even application modules restrict access unless you declare `opens` in `module-info.java`. Workaround for legacy reflection-heavy libraries:

```bash
java --add-opens java.base/java.lang=ALL-UNNAMED -jar app.jar
```

Spring, Hibernate, and Jackson have all had to update to work with stricter module rules — expect this to be an active migration for years.

**Security**:
With reflection, untrusted code can potentially access private state. Java 17 removed the SecurityManager support path — access restrictions are now enforced entirely through the module system and `setAccessible` permissions.

**Modern alternative — `MethodHandle`**:
`MethodHandle` (introduced with invokedynamic in Java 7) is faster than classic reflection, supports JIT inlining better, and is the recommended path for framework authors writing performance-sensitive code. It's also the mechanism lambdas use under the hood.

```java
import java.lang.invoke.*;

MethodHandles.Lookup lookup = MethodHandles.lookup();
MethodType mt = MethodType.methodType(String.class); // return type
MethodHandle mh = lookup.findVirtual(User.class, "getName", mt);

for (int i = 0; i < 1_000_000; i++) {
    String name = (String) mh.invoke(user);
}
```

**Why it's faster:**
- No per-call access check — the handle was validated at lookup time.
- Argument types are known to the JIT via `invokedynamic` — no boxing for primitive handles.
- The JIT can inline through `MethodHandle.invokeExact` because the call site is linked to a specific target.

**Trade-off:** `invokeExact` requires **exact type match** — casts in the call signature must match the `MethodType` exactly. More rigid than reflection, but the rigidity is what lets the JIT optimize.

**Typical numbers:** 5–10 ns per call after JIT warm-up vs 30–80 ns for reflection. Spring 5+ uses `MethodHandle` for property-accessor scenarios.

```java
import java.lang.invoke.*;
import java.util.function.Function;

MethodHandles.Lookup lookup = MethodHandles.lookup();
MethodHandle target = lookup.findVirtual(User.class, "getName",
    MethodType.methodType(String.class));

CallSite site = LambdaMetafactory.metafactory(
    lookup,
    "apply",                                             // interface method
    MethodType.methodType(Function.class),               // factory return
    MethodType.methodType(Object.class, Object.class),   // erased signature
    target,
    MethodType.methodType(String.class, User.class));    // actual signature

@SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
Function<User, String> getNameFn = (Function<User, String>) site.getTarget().invoke();

for (int i = 0; i < 1_000_000; i++) {
    String name = getNameFn.apply(user); // ~1-2 ns, essentially direct
}
```

`LambdaMetafactory` generates a tiny adapter class that calls the target method directly — no reflection, no boxing. The resulting `Function` performs at near-direct-call speed (1–2 ns). This is how Spring 5+ and many modern frameworks call property accessors generated at startup.

**When to use which:**
- **Reflection**: init-time, rare calls, simple code.
- **`MethodHandle`**: hot-path dynamic dispatch where reflection is too slow.
- **`LambdaMetafactory`**: you want near-direct-call performance for a repeatedly-invoked property accessor or method; you build once at startup, use many times.

```java
import java.lang.annotation.*;

@Target({ElementType.METHOD, ElementType.TYPE})  // where it can be applied
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)               // available via reflection
@Documented                                       // include in Javadoc
@Inherited                                        // transfer to subclasses (TYPE only)
public @interface Audited {
    // Parameters — declared as methods with optional defaults
    String value() default "";                   // if only 'value', can be written @Audited("foo")
    AuditLevel level() default AuditLevel.INFO;
    String[] tags() default {};
    boolean async() default false;
}

enum AuditLevel { DEBUG, INFO, WARN, ERROR }

// Usage
@Audited(value = "user-login", level = AuditLevel.WARN, tags = {"security", "auth"})
public void handleLogin(String username) { ... }

// With only 'value':
@Audited("user-logout")
public void handleLogout() { ... }
```

**`@Target` values** (from `ElementType` enum):
- `TYPE` — classes, interfaces, enums, annotations
- `FIELD` — instance/static fields
- `METHOD` — methods
- `PARAMETER` — method parameters
- `CONSTRUCTOR` — constructors
- `LOCAL_VARIABLE` — local variables (rare — retention is usually SOURCE)
- `ANNOTATION_TYPE` — meta-annotations
- `PACKAGE` — packages (via `package-info.java`)
- `TYPE_PARAMETER` (Java 8+) — generic type parameters
- `TYPE_USE` (Java 8+) — any use of a type (for JSR-305-style annotations)

**`@Retention` values**:
- `SOURCE` — discarded by the compiler; not in .class files. Use for annotation-processor-only annotations.
- `CLASS` — in .class files but not loaded by the JVM at runtime. Default if not specified. Rarely useful.
- `RUNTIME` — loaded and available via reflection. Required for Spring, Jackson, JUnit, etc.

**Approach 1: Runtime reflection** (most common — used by Spring, Jackson, Hibernate)

```java
@Aspect
@Component
public class AuditAspect {
    @Around("@annotation(audited)")
    public Object audit(ProceedingJoinPoint pjp, Audited audited) throws Throwable {
        log.info("[AUDIT {}] {} starting", audited.level(), audited.value());
        long start = System.nanoTime();
        try {
            return pjp.proceed();
        } finally {
            log.info("[AUDIT] {} completed in {}ns", audited.value(), System.nanoTime() - start);
        }
    }
}
```

Or raw reflection:

```java
for (Method m : clazz.getDeclaredMethods()) {
    Audited audited = m.getAnnotation(Audited.class);
    if (audited != null) {
        System.out.println(m.getName() + " -> " + audited.value());
    }
}
```

Requires **`@Retention(RUNTIME)`**.

**Approach 2: Compile-time annotation processor** (APT) — used by Lombok, AutoValue, MapStruct, Dagger

