```

Registered via **`META-INF/services/javax.annotation.processing.Processor`**. Runs during `javac`. Can use `@Retention(SOURCE)` because the annotation is only needed at compile time.

**Approach 3: Bytecode manipulation** (AspectJ weaver, ASM, ByteBuddy)

Tools like AspectJ's compile-time weaver process annotations by directly modifying `.class` files after compilation — adding method calls, wrapping methods, generating new classes. This is how `@Transactional` can work without runtime proxies if you use AspectJ weaving instead of Spring AOP proxies.

**Which approach to choose**:

- Runtime reflection: simple, flexible, no extra tooling — slightly slower at runtime
- APT: generates code at compile time — zero runtime overhead, faster than reflection
- Bytecode: most powerful, most complex — used sparingly by advanced tools

`@Target` takes an array of `ElementType` values specifying allowed locations. If you omit `@Target`, the annotation can go anywhere — usually not what you want.

The most-used values:
- `TYPE` — classes, interfaces, enums, annotations (`@Component`, `@Entity`).
- `METHOD` — methods (`@Test`, `@GetMapping`).
- `FIELD` — fields (`@Autowired`, `@JsonIgnore`).
- `PARAMETER` — method parameters (`@RequestParam`, `@Valid`).
- `CONSTRUCTOR` — constructors.
- `LOCAL_VARIABLE` — local variables (rare; `@SuppressWarnings` is an example).
- `ANNOTATION_TYPE` — other annotations (meta-annotations themselves).
- `PACKAGE` — `package-info.java` declarations.
- `TYPE_PARAMETER` (Java 8+) — generic type parameters.
- `TYPE_USE` (Java 8+) — any type use (enables `@NonNull List<@NonNull String>`).

```java
@Target({ElementType.METHOD, ElementType.FIELD})
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
public @interface Audited { }

@Audited
private String fieldOK;

@Audited
public void methodOK() { }

@Audited
public class ClassBad { } // compile error — TYPE not allowed
```
The compiler enforces `@Target` — applying an annotation in a disallowed location is a compile error.

**`@Documented`** — tells the Javadoc tool to include the annotation in generated documentation. Without it, annotations don't appear in Javadoc. Used for API-relevant annotations where callers should see them.
```java
@Documented
@Target(ElementType.METHOD)
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
public @interface Deprecated { ... }
```

**`@Inherited`** — controls whether **subclasses** automatically inherit the annotation from their superclass. **Only applies to class-level annotations; methods and fields don't inherit regardless of this meta-annotation.**
```java
@Inherited
@Target(ElementType.TYPE)
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
public @interface Audited { }

@Audited
class Parent { }
class Child extends Parent { }       // Child IS annotated @Audited (because @Inherited)

child.getClass().getAnnotation(Audited.class); // returns non-null
```
Without `@Inherited`, `Child` would not carry the annotation. Spring's own annotations (like `@Component`) aren't `@Inherited` but Spring handles the discovery separately via its own component scan.

Before Java 8, you couldn't apply the same annotation twice to a single element — you had to wrap in a container annotation manually. Java 8's `@Repeatable` makes this first-class.

```java
// Container annotation
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Target(ElementType.METHOD)
public @interface Schedules {
    Schedule[] value();
}

// Actual repeatable annotation
@Repeatable(Schedules.class)
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Target(ElementType.METHOD)
public @interface Schedule {
    String cron();
}

// Usage: both styles work
@Schedule(cron = "0 0 * * * *")
@Schedule(cron = "0 30 * * * *")
public void job() { }
```

At runtime, reading multiple annotations requires `element.getAnnotationsByType(Schedule.class)` (returns an array) instead of `getAnnotation` (returns the container if multiple). Used by Jakarta Bean Validation (`@Pattern`, `@Size` groups), Spring (`@Scheduled`), and other frameworks.

```java
// 1. Define the annotation (with SOURCE retention — processed only at compile time)
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.SOURCE)
@Target(ElementType.TYPE)
public @interface GenerateBuilder {}
```

```java
// 2. Implement the processor
import javax.annotation.processing.*;
import javax.lang.model.SourceVersion;
import javax.lang.model.element.*;
import javax.tools.JavaFileObject;
import java.io.PrintWriter;
import java.util.Set;

@SupportedAnnotationTypes("com.example.GenerateBuilder")
@SupportedSourceVersion(SourceVersion.RELEASE_17)
public class BuilderProcessor extends AbstractProcessor {

    @Override
    public boolean process(Set<? extends TypeElement> annotations,
                           RoundEnvironment roundEnv) {
        for (Element element : roundEnv.getElementsAnnotatedWith(GenerateBuilder.class)) {
            if (element.getKind() == ElementKind.CLASS) {
                generateBuilder((TypeElement) element);
            }
        }
        return true;  // claim these annotations (other processors won't re-process)
    }

    private void generateBuilder(TypeElement cls) {
        String pkg = processingEnv.getElementUtils().getPackageOf(cls).toString();
        String name = cls.getSimpleName() + "Builder";

        try {
            JavaFileObject file = processingEnv.getFiler().createSourceFile(pkg + "." + name);
            try (PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(file.openWriter())) {
                out.println("package " + pkg + ";");
                out.println("public class " + name + " {");
                // generate fields, setters, build() method reflecting on cls.getEnclosedElements()
                out.println("}");
            }
        } catch (Exception e) {
            processingEnv.getMessager().printMessage(Diagnostic.Kind.ERROR,
                "Failed to generate " + name + ": " + e.getMessage(), cls);
        }
    }
}
```

```
// 3. Register via META-INF/services/javax.annotation.processing.Processor
com.example.BuilderProcessor
```

**Better — use Google's `@AutoService`** to auto-generate the META-INF file:

```java
@AutoService(Processor.class)
@SupportedAnnotationTypes("com.example.GenerateBuilder")
public class BuilderProcessor extends AbstractProcessor { ... }
```

**Use JavaPoet for cleaner generation** instead of raw PrintWriter:

```java
MethodSpec buildMethod = MethodSpec.methodBuilder("build")
    .addModifiers(Modifier.PUBLIC)
    .returns(ClassName.get(cls))
    .addStatement("return new $T($L)", cls, argList)
    .build();

TypeSpec builder = TypeSpec.classBuilder(name)
    .addModifiers(Modifier.PUBLIC)
    .addMethod(buildMethod)
    .build();

JavaFile.builder(pkg, builder).build().writeTo(processingEnv.getFiler());
```
